  <h2>The question</h2>
  <p>
    Gold is often treated as a hedge against a weak US dollar. I tested whether a forecasting model could capture that relationship, using monthly gold prices from the World Bank and the US Dollar Index, aligned on their common dates. That left 165 monthly points from 2006 to 2025, a small dataset for this kind of study, split 80/20 in time order so every model is tested only on dates after its training data.
  </p>

  <h2>Three models, three failure modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SARIMAX</strong>, with the dollar index as the exogenous input, had the lowest AIC of the ARIMA family and followed the trend until 2024. As a linear model it could not follow the exponential rally after that.</li>
    <li><strong>Random forest</strong> scored R² −1.502. Trees cannot predict values outside their training range, so once gold broke its historical high the forecast went flat.</li>
    <li><strong>Neural network</strong> scored R² −1.483 on the raw input: worse than predicting the average.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What changed the answer</h2>
  <p>
    Asset prices depend on their own recent history, so I engineered lagged values and 5-period rolling means and standard deviations for both series. The neural network jumped to R² 0.867 once I also halved its training epochs to stop it overfitting. It was the only model that followed the recent surge, though it still underpredicts the newest points. The random forest improved to R² −0.388 but still flatlined above its training range.
  </p>

